desc:       SCHMELZ Essentials
  version:    2.0.0
  author:     schlacke
  tags:       saturation, colour, EQ, compressor, stereo, clipper, character
  license:    GPL-3.0-or-later
  about:
      # SCHMELZ Essentials
      Analogue-style colouring for single tracks and the mix bus. A
      reduced version of SCHMELZ: filters, saturation, tone, a
      compressor, stereo width, a transformer stage and a soft clipper.
      Every stage can be switched off and then passes the signal
      unchanged (bit-exact).

      Operation: the top half shows the signal flow. Click a block to
      show its controls below, double-click it to switch the module on
      or off. FILTER switches Low Cut and High Cut together and restores
      whichever was on. IN and OUT open the I/O page. Drag a scale to set
      a value, double-click resets it, the mouse wheel makes fine steps.
      Changing a control switches its module on.

      Signal flow:
      Input -> Low Cut -> High Cut -> Grit -> Tone -> Crush -> Field
      -> Iron -> Bass Mono -> Drive -> Weight -> Clipper -> Output

      - Low Cut: high-pass, 10..300 Hz, the frequency is the -3 dB
        point. Slope: Analog (18 dB/oct with a small +0.5 dB bump above
        the corner) or Butterworth 12 / 24 / 48 dB/oct.
      - High Cut: low-pass, 1..30 kHz, the frequency is the -3 dB point
        (minimum phase, limited to 0.45 x sample rate). Slope: Analog
        (18 dB/oct, +0.5 dB below the corner) or Butterworth 12 / 24 /
        48 / 96 dB/oct. It sits before the saturation stages, so they
        never see ultrasonic content.
      - Grit: even-order saturation, oversampled. Amount (0 % is
        bit-transparent). Onset: above this level the amount of colour
        stays constant.
      - Tone: low shelf 30..90 Hz, high shelf 6..20 kHz, +-12 dB. A low
        boost uses a passive-EQ style network with a dip above the
        boost. Dip Position, Width and Depth move the dip lower and make
        it wider or deeper. A high boost adds harmonics (oversampled).
        Cuts are plain EQ.
      - Crush: stereo compressor based on measurements of a hardware
        unit. Feedback design, program-dependent attack, oversampled.
        Mode: Linked, M/S or L/R. Sensitivity: higher = more
        compression. Drive: input stage level (it clips like the
        hardware). Blend: parallel mix. Output: level after Blend.
      - Field: side signal only, the mid is never changed. Depth: side
        shelf below Shuffle. Width: side level. High Width: side shelf
        above Shuffle.
      - Iron: output transformer model. Drive: how hard the core is
        driven. Character: from odd-order saturation towards even-order
        warmth.
      - Analog Tolerance: small left/right differences as in analogue
        hardware. At 0 both channels are identical (mono stays mono).
      - Bass Mono: removes the side signal below the chosen frequency
        (Linkwitz-Riley crossover, the mid gets the matching allpass).
      - Clipper: Drive into a soft clipper (oversampled, with
        anti-aliasing). Depth: how far below the ceiling the curve
        starts to bend (make-up gain is half of it). Ceiling. Blend:
        parallel mix. Weight: single-ended saturation before the clip,
        Post or Pre Drive. Sub Cut: removes sub-bass created by the
        clipping (left stop = off).
      - Output: final gain.

      Latency: 42 samples at 44.1 / 48 kHz, 35 at 88.2 / 96 kHz, none
      above.

      Copyright (C) 2026 schlacke. License: GPL-3.0-or-later.

slider1:0<-12,12,0.1>-Input Gain (dB)
slider2:0<0,1,1{Off,On}>-Low Cut IN
slider3:20<10,300,1:log>-Low Cut (Hz)
slider4:3<0,3,1{Analog,12 dB/oct,24 dB/oct,48 dB/oct}>-Low Cut Slope
