// --- Crush: a compressor modelled on measured hardware (stereo listen-mic compressor) ---
// Mode Linked / M/S / L/R (every position is a 2x2 gain matrix on L, R). A position change
// crossfades from the frozen gain matrix and the model restarts at the gain that played.
// Measured on the hardware (13 takes), fitted stage by stage. Per branch, input stage and
// element run at the inner rate (OS2, CRH_br; the base-rate build aliased at -40 dBc at
// Drive +12):
//  - input stage (Crush Drive, like the hardware's input trim): x * Drive into a hard clip
//    at +5.62 dBFS (the hardware's input stage, +27.6 dBu at 0 dBFS = +22 dBu), residual
//    ADAA. The output takes Drive back out.
//  - feedback compressor: the detector hears the element's input v = s * g. Broadband
//    (1st-order HP 34 Hz: 30 / 60 Hz grab 2.7 / 1.0 dB less, as measured), |h|^3 (reads
//    noise and music ~1 dB hotter than a sine, as the hardware), the louder branch when
//    linked, attack 42 / release 41 ms on |h|^3. Big overshoots are grabbed fast, small
//    ones slowly (90 % after 3..50 ms, as measured).
//  - law: knee 3.8 dB, 6.9:1 just above it, 4.2:1 from 3 dB over (the hardware 6.4 / 4.4:1),
//    range 45 dB. Sensitivity is where a 1-kHz sine gets 1 dB (the knob at 7:00 / 12:00 /
//    5:00 = -10 / -20 / -30 dBFS).
//  - the gain element distorts by its gain reduction only (H3 -28 dBc from ~5 to 13 dB GR,
//    -14 dBc at 26 dB, the same at 100 Hz / 1 kHz / 5 kHz). It is a soft saturator
//    normalised by the signal's own 5-ms RMS (u = v / sqrt(2 ms)),
//    S(u) = u / sqrt(1 + (u / Lim)^2) / kappa (a sine keeps its fundamental), with Lim per
//    GR taken from the measured H3. Noise peaks (u ~ 3) get flattened (hardware: pink noise
//    crest 13.1 -> 10 dB at a steady gain). Plus the measured H2 and residual ADAA. A hot
//    bass moves this element in its rhythm; that is the pumping of hot signals (a high
//    tone gets sidebands at +-100 / 200 / 300 Hz).
//  - fixed make-up from Sensitivity - Drive (pink noise -18 dBFS RMS through the model)
//  - the element's hard regime (second capture, 8 takes): from 22.0 dB gain reduction on
//    the element clamps. A hot bass gets flat peaks and a high tone on it drops ~20 dB for
//    the flat part of every half cycle (the clap of hot signals). It sets in at the same
//    instantaneous gain reduction for 30..160 Hz and for every threshold / input trim (and
//    is where H3 jumps at 1 kHz), so it is a second curve on the same u:
//    C(u) = u up to t1 = k Lh, then t1 + t2 tanh((|u| - t1) / t2) (t2 = (1 - k) Lh),
